Subject: [PATCH] render: hold the last frame across a rotation, then turn it in as Android does

Against Hyprland v0.57.0 (src/render/Renderer.cpp, src/render/OpenGL.cpp).

A monitor transform change lays the layer surfaces out again at once, but
each client draws at its new size only when it gets to it. Until then the
old buffers were drawn stretched and misplaced over the turned output:
stripes, half-empty pages, a home screen's icons in a column, for 200 ms
to a second on a phone (Pixel 7, Omarchy's quickshell).

The hold. After a transform change, renderMonitor() renders nothing until
every mapped layer surface on the monitor has drawn at its new size, or
500 ms have passed: the output keeps scanning out the last frame of the
old orientation, and the first frame of the new one is complete. While
holding, the layer surfaces and the active workspaces get their frame
callbacks once per refresh, since clients draw on them and a skipped
frame would not send them. "Drawn at its new size" is three checks, each
from what was seen on a Pixel 7: the surface's size matches its new
geometry; its buffer does too (Qt resizes the viewport first, with the
old buffer stretched into it, and commits the new buffer after); and a
layer that had to redraw has sent a second buffer at the new size, or its
first has stood for 50 ms (Qt's first frame at a new size can still be
laid out for the old one, which showed as a black bar, and a layer with
nothing more to draw sends no second one).

The turn. Before the hold, the output's last buffer is copied into a
framebuffer of the panel's size (the swapchain reuses that buffer within
a few frames). When the hold ends, the final copy to the output (in
CHyprOpenGLImpl::end()) draws, over black, the new frame turning in from
where the old one sat on the glass to upright over 283 ms (383 ms for a
half turn) on Android's (0.2, 0, 0, 1) curve, and the old frame,
pre-rotated for the new transform so it starts where it was, turning with
it and fading out over 116 ms on Android's alpha-out curve (0.57, 0, 0.71,
0.43) (Android's screen_rotate_*_enter/exit, screen_rotation_alpha_out and
config_screen_rotation_* values). Unlike Android, the new frame is opaque from the start: its fade
in over black read as a dip to black on the phone.

Off unless OMAMOBILE_ROTATION_HOLD=1 is in Hyprland's environment, read
once at startup: a device package turns it on (device-panther's
environment.d), and every other phone keeps Hyprland's own rotation until
the hold has run there. Off, nothing is copied, held or animated.

